text:
anya: Heads-up — running into JSON.parse errors when streaming SSE from the Node SDK. We're getting chunks that split a single JSON object across multiple 'data' events and I'm naively doing `JSON.parse(chunk)` which blows up. Using node-fetch in a server app. Any recommended patterns to reassemble or handle partial JSON and avoid backpressure issues?

sam: Don’t parse per raw chunk. Two common options: 1) use a delimiter (NDJSON / newline-delimited JSON) or 2) parse SSE events (text/event-stream) where each event contains a complete JSON payload. If you can control the server, NDJSON or emitting full JSON in each SSE `data:` event is simplest. :thumbsup:

sam: If you can't change the server, buffer until you have a full object. Example (naive):

```js
let buf = '';
res.body.on('data', (chunk) => {
  buf += chunk.toString();
  let idx;
  while ((idx = buf.indexOf('
')) >= 0) {
    const line = buf.slice(0, idx).trim();
    buf = buf.slice(idx + 1);
    if (!line) continue;
    try {
      const obj = JSON.parse(line);
      handle(obj);
    } catch (e) {
      // not a full line/JSON yet, put it back and wait for more
      buf = line + '
' + buf;
      break;
    }
  }
});
```

sam: Another robust option is to use a streaming JSON parser lib (clarinet, oboe, jsonparse) which incrementally parses tokens and emits objects when complete. Avoid trying to reassemble braces by hand unless tiny.

mike: Also watch backpressure: Node's http/stream API can be paused/resumed. If you do heavy processing per message, call `res.body.pause()` while you process and `res.body.resume()` when ready. If you're on a WHATWG stream (fetch in newer node), use a TransformStream/async iterator with controlled concurrency. Example for Node Readable: `res.body.pause(); processMsg().then(()=> res.body.resume())`.

mike: If using `fetch` in Node 18+ where `res.body` is a stream.Readable, you can do: `for await (const chunk of res.body) { /* parse/accumulate */ }` but this doesn't give you an explicit pause API — instead, control concurrency in your parser (e.g., await processing inside the loop).

sam: For SSE specifically, the server sends `data: ...

` blocks. The canonical parsing approach: split on `

` (event boundary), then extract lines starting with `data: ` and join them. That gives you complete payloads to JSON.parse. Minimal parser: `buffer += chunk; while ((i = buffer.indexOf('

')) !== -1) { event = buffer.slice(0,i); buffer = buffer.slice(i+2); /* extract data lines */ }`.

anya: That helps. Curious about memory: if stream stalls and we buffer a lot, how to avoid unbounded memory growth?

sam: Set a max buffer size and drop/emit an error if exceeded. Also set the Readable highWaterMark appropriately on the HTTP client or use `res.body.pause()` to apply backpressure. For the SDK helper we should document defaults (e.g., 1MB max buffer) and expose config.

mike: For backpressure-friendly SDK, we should expose an async iterator that yields parsed messages and only reads more from the socket when the consumer `await`s the next item. Implementation idea: internally use a Transform stream that collects SSE events into full JSON objects, then `Readable.from(async function*(){ for await (const obj of parser) yield obj })`. This gives natural backpressure since the consumer controls the `for await` pace.

sam: TL;DR recommendations: 1) prefer NDJSON or full JSON per SSE event; 2) use an incremental JSON parser if server can't change; 3) implement pause/resume or async-iterator wrapper in SDK to respect backpressure; 4) add configurable max-buffer.

anya: Awesome. @sam @mike can one of you sketch a tiny helper util we can include in the Node SDK to parse SSE -> JSON with backpressure? I'll open a PR and attach test vectors.

mike: I can draft a minimal `sseParser` that emits parsed objects and exposes an async iterator. Will post a WIP PR tomorrow morning.

